WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:04.941
In this lesson you'll learn how SynthID text

2
00:00:04.941 --> 00:00:09.957
hides a watermark in an AI's word choices.

3
00:00:09.957 --> 00:00:11.501
Nothing is added to the text.

4
00:00:11.501 --> 00:00:14.844
Instead, it tilts which word gets picked,

5
00:00:14.844 --> 00:00:17.306
and only when the model had a real choice.

6
00:00:17.306 --> 00:00:19.245
So the writing reads the same,

7
00:00:19.245 --> 00:00:22.414
exact answers stay exact, and anyone holding

8
00:00:22.414 --> 00:00:27.085
the secret key can check for the mark.

9
00:00:27.085 --> 00:00:29.604
Say the model is finishing this sentence:

10
00:00:29.604 --> 00:00:31.961
the tea house sits at the edge of the blank.

11
00:00:31.961 --> 00:00:33.296
It has its odds.

12
00:00:33.296 --> 00:00:35.398
Garden, thirty-four percent.

13
00:00:35.398 --> 00:00:37.035
River, twenty.

14
00:00:37.035 --> 00:00:38.602
Forest, sixteen.

15
00:00:38.602 --> 00:00:39.972
And a few more.

16
00:00:39.972 --> 00:00:42.665
Normally a random number would pick one.

17
00:00:42.665 --> 00:00:45.893
SynthID runs a tournament instead.

18
00:00:45.893 --> 00:00:47.820
It fills the bracket with contestants

19
00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:49.469
drawn from those odds,

20
00:00:49.469 --> 00:00:52.789
so likely words get more contestants.

21
00:00:52.789 --> 00:00:55.343
Before the first match, every possible word

22
00:00:55.343 --> 00:00:58.408
gets a plus or a minus for each round.

23
00:00:58.408 --> 00:01:00.684
Those are its round assignments.

24
00:01:00.684 --> 00:01:02.843
They come from the secret key

25
00:01:02.843 --> 00:01:05.862
and the four tokens before the blank,

26
00:01:05.862 --> 00:01:08.045
so the same key and the same four tokens

27
00:01:08.045 --> 00:01:10.309
always give the same assignments.

28
00:01:10.309 --> 00:01:14.315
In each match, a plus beats a minus.

29
00:01:14.315 --> 00:01:19.411
Two pluses, or two minuses, and it's a coin flip.

30
00:01:19.411 --> 00:01:21.385
The last contestant standing

31
00:01:21.385 --> 00:01:23.382
is the word that gets written.

32
00:01:23.382 --> 00:01:26.459
Here, river had the better assignments,

33
00:01:26.459 --> 00:01:28.900
and river won.

34
00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:30.502
With these four tokens,

35
00:01:30.502 --> 00:01:33.242
the assignments favour river.

36
00:01:33.242 --> 00:01:35.680
Change the tokens before the blank,

37
00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:38.432
and every assignment is redrawn.

38
00:01:38.432 --> 00:01:40.568
Now garden is favoured.

39
00:01:40.568 --> 00:01:42.114
Now forest.

40
00:01:42.716 --> 00:01:45.409
Across thousands of different sentences,

41
00:01:45.409 --> 00:01:47.813
each word still wins about as often

42
00:01:47.813 --> 00:01:49.577
as the model wanted.

43
00:01:49.577 --> 00:01:52.155
The real system plays thirty rounds,

44
00:01:52.155 --> 00:01:54.802
not three, so in any one spot

45
00:01:54.802 --> 00:01:57.704
the assignments nearly decide the word.

46
00:01:57.704 --> 00:02:00.363
But averaged over everything the model writes,

47
00:02:00.363 --> 00:02:02.349
the odds are its own.

48
00:02:02.349 --> 00:02:05.776
That's why the quality holds.

49
00:02:05.776 --> 00:02:08.504
Now ask: what's the capital of France?

50
00:02:08.504 --> 00:02:10.861
The model is almost certain it's Paris.

51
00:02:10.861 --> 00:02:13.926
So almost every contestant is Paris.

52
00:02:13.926 --> 00:02:16.608
Paris plays Paris, and Paris wins,

53
00:02:16.608 --> 00:02:18.559
whatever the assignments say.

54
00:02:18.559 --> 00:02:20.497
The watermark had nothing to act on,

55
00:02:20.497 --> 00:02:23.226
so the answer stays exact.

56
00:02:23.226 --> 00:02:26.384
The same goes for a web address, a file path,

57
00:02:26.384 --> 00:02:29.089
or a quote you asked for word for word.

58
00:02:29.089 --> 00:02:31.922
But ask for a city famous for its food,

59
00:02:31.922 --> 00:02:36.044
and Paris is one good answer among many.

60
00:02:36.044 --> 00:02:37.483
Now the assignments matter,

61
00:02:37.483 --> 00:02:39.573
and the watermark does its usual work.

62
00:02:39.573 --> 00:02:40.920
It's not about the word.

63
00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:43.442
It's about whether there was a choice.

64
00:02:43.442 --> 00:02:46.532
The checker never needs the model.

65
00:02:47.134 --> 00:02:49.816
It reads the text one token at a time.

66
00:02:49.816 --> 00:02:51.790
For each token, it takes the four tokens

67
00:02:51.790 --> 00:02:54.204
before it, adds the secret key,

68
00:02:54.204 --> 00:02:57.351
and recreates that token's round assignments.

69
00:02:57.351 --> 00:02:59.603
Then it counts the pluses.

70
00:02:59.603 --> 00:03:02.029
Text written without the watermark

71
00:03:02.029 --> 00:03:04.293
lands near half pluses.

72
00:03:04.293 --> 00:03:07.068
Watermarked text leans toward plus,

73
00:03:07.068 --> 00:03:08.612
and the longer the text,

74
00:03:08.612 --> 00:03:11.352
the less that lean can be luck.

75
00:03:11.352 --> 00:03:12.908
Check with the wrong key,

76
00:03:12.908 --> 00:03:15.369
and the assignments are just noise.

77
00:03:15.369 --> 00:03:16.867
Back to half.

78
00:03:16.867 --> 00:03:20.535
That's why only the key holder can check.

79
00:03:20.535 --> 00:03:21.952
And a hit only says

80
00:03:21.952 --> 00:03:24.518
the model was probably involved.

81
00:03:24.518 --> 00:03:27.378
It can't say who wrote the text.

82
00:03:27.378 --> 00:03:28.748
Change one token,

83
00:03:28.748 --> 00:03:31.395
and five tokens get new assignments:

84
00:03:31.395 --> 00:03:34.181
that one, and the four after it,

85
00:03:34.181 --> 00:03:37.421
because their previous tokens changed.

86
00:03:37.421 --> 00:03:39.406
Their evidence is gone.

87
00:03:39.406 --> 00:03:42.716
Every other token keeps its evidence.

88
00:03:43.179 --> 00:03:46.232
Swapping in synonyms does the most damage,

89
00:03:46.232 --> 00:03:49.448
because synonyms are exactly the free choices

90
00:03:49.448 --> 00:03:51.852
where the watermark lives.

91
00:03:51.852 --> 00:03:54.359
Asking the same model to reword the text

92
00:03:54.359 --> 00:03:58.237
just writes new watermarked text.

93
00:03:58.237 --> 00:04:01.546
A rewrite by a model without the key picks

94
00:04:01.546 --> 00:04:05.044
every word again, and the watermark is gone.

95
00:04:05.044 --> 00:04:07.598
So length is everything.

96
00:04:07.598 --> 00:04:11.453
A short email can lose its mark to a few edits.

97
00:04:11.453 --> 00:04:13.740
A whole book keeps it through a lot of them.

98
00:04:13.740 --> 00:04:15.481
Three things to keep.

99
00:04:15.481 --> 00:04:19.057
The watermark lives in free word choices.

100
00:04:19.057 --> 00:04:21.646
Exact answers stay exact,

101
00:04:21.646 --> 00:04:23.573
because there was nothing to choose.

102
00:04:23.573 --> 00:04:25.663
And the checker counts pluses,

103
00:04:25.663 --> 00:04:28.391
so long text keeps the mark, and a rewrite

104
00:04:28.391 --> 00:04:31.539
by a model without the key removes it.
